# One line, imperative, no trailing period. The subject is read in a git log
# and in a release note, so it has to say what changed for a person.
#
#   feat(access): strip [!SECRET] blocks during render
#   fix(search): rank exact title matches before body matches
#   chore: initialise Go module and tooling
#
# Types:  feat, fix, docs, test, refactor, build, ci, chore, perf
# Scopes: vault, index, search, access, auth, render, plugin, http, sse, cli,
#         dnd5e. Leave the scope off only when no scope fits.
#
# The body answers why, not what. A diff already says what changed. If the
# change is a bug fix, the body is the sentence a player would recognise.
#
# Every commit that touches internal/, cmd/, plugins/, migrations/ or web/ must
# change CHANGELOG.md in the same commit. That is the house rule; it is not
# negotiable per commit and the CI check will fail the push.
#
# Breaking changes: add "!" before the colon and a "BREAKING CHANGE:" paragraph
# describing the migration.

